In the inclusive, more structured environment I envision, the patch
was done well the first time around, because of the benefits of
visibility and collaboration - more sets of eyes and more input and
more developer time available.

People can write or rewrite anything they want for fun.  I don't see
that as pertinent to the specifics of nethack.


> ... Finally, most people
> post YANIs in an undeveloped English form first, and let everyone discuss
> them, and if there's already a similar patch, they're pointed to it at
> that point. If after that they still want to go off and make their own
> patch, more power to 'em.


It might work that way sometimes, but often it does not.  Informal,
unstructured commentary on r.g.r.n, which might not be read at all by
a nethack coder, is no substitute for a good collaborative, probably
web-based project.

 
> > The lack of central coordination and a central repository results in
> > many coding conflicts and abandoned projects.  This is magnified at
> > the time of each official nethack release, when independent patches
> > may be superseded, or have to be tediously reworked and reintegrated
> > into the game, often over much time.
> >
> > This isn't an ecology.
> 
>    Um, no. It's also not an orangutan or a big pizza pie. It's NetHack,
> a massive ancient computer program that people run for fun.


You're evading my point by making light of the concern.  Yep, in the
end it's about having fun.  But it's also about time and effort and
even getting recognition for your work and knowing it is being used.
Much nethack coding out there fritters away because of the haphazard
nature of it all - few people ever see it and the dozens of patch and
fan sites that might collect it get to be a meaningless jumble fairly
quickly.

I wonder how many of the current devteam members would accept writing
code for nethack very long if they were in the shoes of the average
aficionado - spend finite personal time writing something you think is
valuable, then throw it out there into the morass and hope that it
might be noticed or used by other players, or by some nethack VIP who
doesn't necessarily even feel an obligation to acknowledge your
effort.  I think the community has matured enough that it doesn't need
this secrecy/gatekeeper bs any more.

 
> > Of course not everything would be accepted.  That's not what I'm
> > saying.  But overall, more new ideas would be integrated.  There would
> > be a much higher degree of organization and communication.  Work would
> > be less duplicated and accomplished more quickly.  Releases wouldn't
> > be glacial.
> 
>    Have you considered that maybe some people /like/ playing the same
> game, or a substantially similar game, for years on end?  NetHack is
> not Microsoft Office. We have no need for "work" to be "accomplished";
> that's not even a meaningful metaphor with NetHack, because it's already
> /in/ a working state. (Modulo the known-bugs list, of course, but it
> doesn't sound like you're talking about bugfix releases here.)


*Some* people like playing the same game for years, maybe even *many*
people, but to emphasize only that ignores the other considerable,
overlapping group of people who want to see change and improvement,
and maybe want to contribute to it.  That's evidenced on the group
here almost every day.

You can keep making fun of my seriousness about this issue, but it's
not a reasoned response.  For example, "work" has a broader meaning
than just dreary stuff you don't like to do at the office.


> > Teamwork accomplishes amazing things.  The lone wolf programmer model
> > isn't delivering here.
> 
>    Which do you mean --- the "lone wolf" DevTeam isn't releasing new
> versions, or the "lone wolf" Usenet community isn't releasing patches?


Nethack aficionados who want to write code or contribute in some way
are forced to act as "lone wolves", because of the lack of a publicly
available collaborative nethack project.

The devteam is a "team" but it's a closed team.

Slashem may be somewhat more collaborative, but is substantially
different from nethack and is in no danger of becoming "official"
nethack, and is significantly less popular and well-known.  Most
people want to contribute to the real deal.  (And by saying this, I am
in no way putting down Slashem).


>    In short: The only thing I'd like to see different about the DevTeam's
